# Every text file checks out with LF, whatever core.autocrlf the clone has (#255).
#
# Git for Windows' installer defaults to core.autocrlf=true, which converts text
# files to CRLF on checkout unless a rule here says otherwise. The nginx image
# sources frontend/nginx/*.envsh at start (`. "$f"` under `set -e`), and a CRLF
# copy fails to parse, so the web container exits and the documented install
# command fails. CI cannot see it: every runner checks out with LF.
# backend/tests/test_line_endings.py asserts the effective attribute on the files
# the image consumes. Binary detection is git's own (text=auto); the explicit
# line below only makes the one tracked binary type unambiguous.
* text=auto eol=lf
*.png binary

# The golden archive (#303) is an export, byte for byte: its CSVs end every record
# in CRLF, and the rule above would rewrite them to LF on `git add`. `-text` keeps
# git's hands off the bytes and still diffs them as text.
# backend/tests/test_golden_archive.py asserts the effective attribute.
backend/tests/fixtures/golden/archive/** -text
